A high-volume sender should be built around provider limits and consent, not around evading filtering. For an opt-in campaign, a worker can select active subscribers, choose a configured brand identity, and retry only transient transport failures. A provider-neutral sketch looks like this:

for batch in batches(active_subscribers, size=500):
    for message in batch:
        result = api.send(
            sender=message.brand_identity,
            subject=message.approved_subject,
            recipient=message.address,
            body=message.body,
        )
        record(result.message_id, result.status)
        if result.is_transient:
            retry_with_backoff(message, limit=3)
        if result.is_permanent:
            suppress(message.address)

Do not interpret this as permission to maintain a collection of accounts or to change subjects until a filter lets the message through. A sender identity should be tied to a verified domain and a real brand. If an identity is blocked, pause the campaign, inspect complaints and authentication, and contact the provider. Moving the same queue to another account would defeat the provider’s abuse control. Similarly, subject variants should be approved for truthful communication and testing, not used to disguise one unsolicited offer as many unrelated messages.

Before enqueueing, verify a durable consent record containing source, time, notice version, topic, and withdrawal status. Query a suppression service for unsubscribes, complaints, hard bounces, and manual holds immediately before each batch. Every message needs accurate sender information, a working unsubscribe link, a preference center, and required contact details. Use a sandbox with reserved addresses to test idempotency, rate handling, event processing, and automatic pausing. Keep credentials, recipient data, and production routing out of the test. This architecture can support millions of genuinely subscribed recipients, but it does not make unsolicited distribution or filter evasion acceptable. The owner of a campaign should be able to pause every worker immediately and explain why the audience is eligible. A provider block is an abuse signal, not a routing problem. Keep the sandbox and production credentials, recipient stores, and sender configuration strictly separate. Keep the decision log available for compliance review and test suppression changes before any public send.
